A||||12||Homicidal||Is this mechanic lying or should I do it?????||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-28-2000||03:29 PM||||I took my car into my girlfriend's uncle and he said my clutch problem was due to a worn pressure plate.  He said nothing else was wrong and the cylinders were fine!<P>I just took my car into a transmission place to replace the clutch and they are telling me now that by VISUAL inspection the pressure place doesn't look that worn and it is probably burned master or slave cylinder.<P>I feel I am being taken, so I told him to just put the car back together with the new plate and clutch.<P>Should I trust what they say or does it sound like a scam?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||mwbackus||04-28-2000||04:05 PM||||Even though no one seems to pay attention to my posts, I will state for the record that I believe they were both 50% correct; your hydraulic system is fine, and your clutch did not need replacement.  <BR>Either the system was improperly bled or the release lever was cracked/loose/striped where it attaches to the clutch shaft.  Clear enough for ya!<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||04-28-2000||04:15 PM||||Okay, my girlfriend's uncle bled the system fully and then checked it out.  He told me it was the pressure plate FOR SURE.  Why did I beleive him?  Because a) Everyone kept telling me to just take it into a mechanic and b)I don't know enough to DIS-beleive him.<P>So I think what I will do is IF this transmission place tries to tell me that it's my cylinders, I will take my car back and drop it in my driveway and fix it my d@mn self, with the help of a bunch of gearheads.<P>I just don't know enough to know who is telling me the truth and who isn't.<P>I am not saying you aren't, but how can YOU be sure that's what it is?  I have had TONS of people telling me "I'm positive that this or that is it, FOR SURE."<P>But everybody has a different story.<P>But you say they were both 50% correct.  If the system was bled professionally but it still didn't work, what does that mean?<P>Thanks for the help man!||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||04-28-2000||04:57 PM||||Hey Homicidal,<P>It is REALLY hard to diagnose problems from a written description. It's hard enough first hand. <P>Your profile doesn't say what part of the world you're in. It might be that there is a "gearhead" forum member right in your town that could take a first hand look???<P>Not to question your gf's uncle, but how much does he know about Fiero clutch hydraulics? I consider myself a pretty good amuteur mechanic (25 yrs experience). Trying to bleed the Fiero clutch is one of the more challenging things I have done recently. If you try to do it like you do brakes, you will probably still have air in it.<P>Did the shop already drop the tranny? <P>The thing mwbackus is talking about is the actuator assembly between the slave cylinder and the pressure plate. These have a tendency to crack or slip. When this happens, the hydraulics and the clutch/pressure plate may be fine but the movement gets "lost" in the actuator and the clutch does not work.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||04-28-2000||05:02 PM||||To futher confuse the issue... My most recent clutch problem turned out to be loose bolts on the slave cylinder mounting brackets. Wasn't real noticable what was wrong until someone pushed the padal while I watched. The slave cylinder and bracket moved back instead of moving the arm.<P>Driving it... it acted just like air in the system, bad actuator arm or bad pressure plate.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||04-29-2000||03:24 AM||||Well, they put the new clutch assembly and then said "Nope, your master and slave cylinder are gone."<P>BULL$HIT!  I have written proof from my gf's uncle those cylinders were perfect and I had perfect hydraulic pressure in my car.  He had my clutch feeling perfect, but it just would not disengage.  He even manually pushed the lever back and it still wouldn't disengage.  So he concluded it was the pressure plate or throwout bearing or something along those lines.<P>But he said the master and slave were fine.  These other mechanics want $400 to replace the cylinders!!!!!!!!!!!!!! <P>NO, even if it WAS the cylinders, I could do the job MYSELF and save $200!  <P>So I think they are trying to milk me of my money because they quoted such a low price for the installation.      ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-29-2000||09:25 AM||||There are times when I think most mechanics charge WAY too much money. Then there are other times when I'm covered with dirt and grease, been struggling most of the day trying to pull some little stupid gizmo off the car, then a bolt breaks, and I just bashed my knuckles...I get pissed and let it sit for a couple days and then start all over again. Now those times, I often feel they don't get enough money.<P>That said, the Fiero clutch hydraulics can be a huge nightmare when it comes to repair or bleeding. Most non-Fiero mechanics don't know much about them either. Look at it this way, if you bite the bullet and get everything repaired, you won't have any clutch headache for a long while, you'll send some mechanic's kid to college, and you'll get Princess off your back...for a while...LOL.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||04-29-2000||06:56 PM||||This is one of the reasons why I have 2 <B>automatics</B> parked in my driveway. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Racer 1||04-30-2000||02:47 PM||||You should have called a live phsycic 1-900 number then you could have gotten a third parties view????||||reg||14
Z||000009||GT Bastard||04-30-2000||05:20 PM||||Damn, you guys are starting to give me second thoughts about my auto to manual swap..||||reg||8
Z||000010||Ozzy||04-30-2000||10:51 PM||||Na...are you kidding? Stick is much more fun in these cars.<P>I had my fun with the Coupe on the master and slave cyls. I did the ATF thing and, yes my rubber is sloooowly melting but I'm playing ostrich until...<BR>But the clutch has been working fine. I used the gravity feed bleeding too.<P>Homicidal...you sound like you are getting <B>homicidal</B>! Chill man. I know it's easy for me to say but like Batboy says...investing money in your clutch system now means that this work is out of the way and you can look forward to the <I>other</I> million or so things you will have to spend $$$ on.<P>Personally, If I have the slightest chance of doing the work myself, with the help of the good folks here on the forum, I DO it myself. I don't have much spare time but I make time 'cause I'd sooner screw it up myself than have a hard on for someone else. Besides, I take the time necessary. Shops are in bussiness to make MONEY! Generally speaking, they don't give a rats a$$ about your pride and joy. They care about jammin' it out the door A.S.A.P. so they can do the next one. I cringe watching someone just lift my car off the ground on a shop lift!<P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 04-30-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000011||DJRice||05-01-2000||11:01 AM||||First off, unless you know how to bleed a Fiero clutch, chances are you didnt bleed it right...<P>Second, What year is the car?  Will a magnet stick to your clutch pedal?  If not, chances are you have a bent clutch pedal which will magnify any other anomalies in the clutch system.<P>Third, it seems that with transmissions, the first guess by the mechanic is always off by about 50% as far as cost goes.  There is always something more gone that costs more money.  If you had let them replace the cylinders,  chances are they would have come back and said "Well, it looks like we need to replace the pressure plate too..."<P>Bottom line:  Its like Batboy said, If the mechanic can do the job, let him do it.  When he returns the car to you with a fully operational clutch, just be glad it wasnt your busted bloody knuckles all weekend long that pulled it off.  Be glad it wasnt you who had to fight and fight to get the tranny and engine properly aligned.  Be glad it wasnt you who learned Fiero Clucth bleeding procedures via "trial by fire".<P><BR>Wait, what am I saying, Thats the fun part!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||mshill||05-01-2000||04:38 PM||||I only partially agree with Batboy and DJRice.  Whatever the mechanic fixes will not be the only problem.  The car is old and many things are wearing out all at different stages.  You will always break the weakest part of a system first, but make no mistake about it others will follow.<P>Where I diverge from their views is WHO should do the work.  I am not a mechanic, but I do all of my own work, unless I lack the tools to do so.  A good example is I just finished rebuilding my 2.8L V6 and did everything except the machine work and the four wheel alighment when it was done.  No offense to you real mechanics out there, but this is not rocket science.  It is all tools and experience.  Most of the tools can be had for free (AutoZone Tool Lending program) and the experience can be had through the Haynes and Chilton manuals as well as people here.<P>In the long run, I trust my work as much as any mechanics, and it cost me only parts, time, and a little skin.  I learned a lot and now have an intimate knowledge of the insides of my favorite toy.||||reg||1
